<p>Tens of thousands of people marched in central London on Saturday in two rival demonstrations: the “Unite the Kingdom” rally organized by far-right activist Stephen Yaxley-Lennon, known as Tommy Robinson, and a pro-Palestinian Nakba Day rally that also drew anti-racism and anti-fascism campaigners. The Metropolitan Police deployed about 4,000 officers, along with horses, dogs, drones and helicopters, to keep the groups apart while London also hosted the FA Cup Final at Wembley. Police said Sunday they made 43 arrests during the protest operation: 20 linked to Unite the Kingdom, 12 linked to the Nakba Day rally, and 11 either unrelated or not yet tied to either group. The Met said 11 arrests involved alleged hate crime offences, including nine linked to Unite the Kingdom and two linked to the Nakba Day rally; it was also investigating seven further alleged hate crime offences from the Nakba Day protest. The Met estimated about 60,000 people joined the Unite the Kingdom march and between 15,000 and 20,000 attended the Nakba Day rally. Officers said serious clashes did not occur, while four officers were assaulted and six were subjected to hate crime offences during Saturday’s operation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/tzr7zDPZ7IyGWGab0HPTaq4nz3cQLX2dW_V9qZjfDKOupakBMSPuxifaeeG3AKdmCTuorrShe6asoCGqJ1lOUiIZIiA9gD4GtCBVaO3Ulida' alt='Pro-Palestinian demonstrators carry flags and placards along Piccadilly during a London rally held the same day as the Unite the Kingdom march.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Security cost: Reports citing the Metropolitan Police said the operation cost about £4.5 million and included armoured vehicles.</li><li>Facial recognition: Police said live facial recognition led to three arrests, and that all three suspects were wanted for failing to appear in court and were not affiliated with the protests.</li><li>Government action: The British government barred 11 people it described as foreign far-right agitators from entering the country to address the Unite the Kingdom protest.</li><li>Prior rally: A previous Robinson-led protest in September featured a video address by US tech billionaire Elon Musk; more than 20 people were arrested and police were still seeking more than 50 suspects.</li></ul><blockquote>Immigration’s the main concern - Christine Turner</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Prime Minister Keir Starmer: Starmer said before the march that organisers of Unite the Kingdom were “peddling hate and division, plain and simple,” according to a Reuters-based report. (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330459089/tens-of-thousands-march-in-london-in-separate-immigration-propalestinian-protests'>Aaj News</a>)</li><li>Stephen Yaxley-Lennon, known as Tommy Robinson: Robinson posted a video from the protest saying, “Keir Starmer, the country’s awake, your days are numbered,” and separately claimed millions attended the march. (<a href='https://www.irishnews.com/news/uk/tens-of-thousands-of-people-attend-two-protests-as-31-arrested-GX4MBZNDU5N5DEPY5ZYLXFYIHU/'>The Irish News</a>)</li><li>Metropolitan Police: Before the demonstrations, the force said it would make “the most assertive possible use of our powers” as it prepared for what it called its biggest public-order operation in years. (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330459089/tens-of-thousands-march-in-london-in-separate-immigration-propalestinian-protests'>Aaj News</a>)</li><li>Stand Up to Racism: The group organised an anti-fascism march that joined the pro-Palestinian Nakba Day rally and said it aimed to counter the far-right gathering. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/2000815/london-streets-swell-with-rival-protests-over-gaza-immigration'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/british-police-detail-arrests-after-far-right-rally-counter-demo'>British police detail arrests after far-right rally, counter-demo</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn